  "language": "en",
  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "A radar finds distant objects by sending out radio waves and listening for their echoes. This works in darkness because radio echoes do not need visible light. The radar's transmitter creates a short radio pulse and its antenna sends that pulse into space. The pulse travels outward at the speed of light until it reaches an object, such as an aircraft. Some radio energy reflects from the aircraft and returns toward the radar antenna. The radar measures the echo's round-trip time to calculate distance. Multiplying half that time by the speed of light gives the objects range. A rotating antenna repeats this process in different directions, building a map of echoes around the radar. So radar locates objects by timing radio wave echoes rather than relying on visible light.",
  "mean_word_probability": 0.9661675377533986,
